1. Introduction

Touchscreen interfaces are employed across a wide range of technologies, from mobile phones to in-car infotainment systems. Growth in these markets has been rapid: the number of users of touchscreen phones increased by 159% in the year August 2008–2009 [1] , with a corresponding increase of 49% in worldwide smartphone sales between Q1 2009 and Q1 2010 [2] . Similarly, the availability of touchscreen-based in-car systems in the US market increased from 44% to 55% for the 2009 model year [3] . These trends reflect the increased functionality present in today’s technology and the flexibility afforded by a touchscreen interface.

Touchscreens offer practical benefits in use [4] : input is direct as the display and input are co-located and the simple mode of interaction, pointing, is familiar even to novice users [5] . A major drawback with ‘traditional’ touchscreens however is the lack of haptic feedback, both tactile and kinaesthetic, provided by the screen’s surface. This places significant demand on visual attention [6] which raises potential issues with respect to safety; road accident study data indicate that 60% of crashes, near-crashes and incidents can be attributed to glances away from the forward roadway [7] .

The provision of feedback is an essential part of the interaction experience. Norman [8] describes the ‘Gulf of Evaluation’ – the effort that a user must exert in order to interpret the state of the system and determine the extent to which expectations and intentions have been met. Feedback that is relevant, easily interpreted and clear helps to reduce this effort and minimise frustration, which the user may otherwise project onto a system through a negative perception of usability. Touchscreen devices have previously relied on visual feedback, again emphasising the visually-dominant nature of the technology. Removing visual feedback causes a significant increase in input error rates [9] , as with physical keyboards [10] and mobile phone keypads [11] .

Touchscreen design guidelines [12] state that visual feedback should be immediate in order to reassure the user that their input has registered; however no literature was found regarding the effects of latency in touchscreen visual feedback. The study of visual feedback latency in virtual environments has been more comprehensive (e.g. [13] , [14] ), showing reductions in task performance and increased error rates; however the nature of the visual feedback in these cases is continuous rather than discrete and thus not directly analogous to touchscreen use.
1.1. Multimodal interaction

The benefits of multimodal interaction have been widely studied. A meta-analysis of 43 studies which compared visual to visual–auditory and visual–tactile feedback [15] showed improvements in reaction times and performance scores over visual feedback alone, although no overall improvement was seen in error rates. This suggests that moving workload from the visual to the haptic channel can reduce cognitive demand.

Incorporating haptic feedback into a touchscreen interface enables users to receive feedback in both modalities employed in interaction. A number of studies have been conducted which specifically investigate the effects of haptic feedback in touchscreens: in mobile devices, haptic feedback was shown to reduce error rates for entry and selection tasks, reduce task completion time [16] and improve workload [17] . Lee and Spence [18] assessed the benefits of bimodal and trimodal feedback in a high-workload scenario, using a driving-themed reaction time test and touchscreen-based telephone secondary tasks. Their results showed that time taken to react to the driving task and to complete the telephone tasks were both reduced when trimodal (visual, haptic and auditory) feedback was enabled, with a corresponding reduction in subjective workload.

Further subjective benefits were seen in other studies: Lee et al. [19] investigated the effect of multimodal feedback on performance in older adults, using a bench-top emulation of a mobile phone interface. Results showed that while visual–auditory and visual–auditory–haptic feedback showed improvements in task performance, only trimodal feedback yielded reductions in perceived task difficulty. In the context of automotive use, Serafin et al. [20] showed that users preferred bimodal (visual plus auditory or haptic) feedback over visual alone, with tri-modal feedback strongly most preferred. Pitts et al. [21] conducted trials based on automotive touchscreen use cases scenarios in a simulated driving environment, with combinations of visual, auditory and haptic feedback. Again, tri-modal feedback was strongly most preferred by users, showing improvements in subjective difficulty rating, user confidence and driving task interference over visual feedback alone.
1.2. The nature of touchscreen interaction

Acting as both display and input, and in the absence of auditory feedback, touchscreen interaction employs the visual and haptic senses. For a menu-based interaction, the user employs target search visual scanning behaviour in order to locate the required target, the number of glances required depending upon interface design, expected target location and user experience [22] . Haptic interaction provides the input to the touchscreen and occurs once the target has been located, with feedback stimuli produced at this point. Further visual interaction may occur post-input through confirmation glances, employing supervisory control visual scanning behaviour. For the purposes of this study we define glances made prior to successful activation of the target as primary glances and those made after activation as secondary glances.

The nature of the haptic element of interaction is dependent on the feedback stimulus. Unlike vision, the haptic system makes use of both sensory (tactile) information and the movements required to obtain it [23] . Jones and Lederman [24] differentiate between passive tactile sensing, whereby the stationary hand is stimulated by either a stationary or moving object; and active haptic sensing, where the hand acts upon an otherwise static object. For a simple button-press action, haptic interaction with a touch screen at the point of contact may be considered passive, as there are no surface textures or features from which to discern information. Even with the introduction of vibrotactile feedback upon activation the interaction may still be considered passive; Jones and Lederman again draw the distinction between a tactile display and a haptic display which incorporates kinaesthetic feedback. However, transient feedback stimuli, designed to replicate mechanical switch-feel characteristics and invoke the paradigm of switch use, may induce a sensation of longitudinal displacement [25] ; the user ‘feels’ that the screen moves inwards when pressed. In this sense, one may argue that the perception of kinaesthesia is sufficient for the interaction to be considered active and hence the description of the display as ‘haptic’ is appropriate. The issue of the lack of tactile cues to assist in target identification and location still persists, however.
1.3. Study outline

While the literature discussed above offers evidence for the benefits of multimodal interaction over visual alone, the nature of the visual–haptic interaction and how it relates to real-world applications is unclear. A study was therefore conducted to investigate the interaction of visual and haptic feedback in an automotive touchscreen use case, using a simulated driving environment. Objective measures of visual behaviour, task and driving performance were employed, along with a range of subjective measures of user response. A repeated-measures experiment design was used in which participants performed touchscreen trials in the presence and absence of both visual and haptic feedback, while engaged in a driving task. In addition, an intermediate visual feedback condition was assessed whereby the visual stimulus is delayed; this was included both to investigate the extent to which the visual element dominates touchscreen interaction and to represent feedback latency issues found in some production vehicle interfaces.
2. Research questions and hypotheses

The study aims to address the following research questions:

•

    Does the introduction of haptic feedback affect objective driving performance?
•

    Does haptic feedback affect the visual workload demand of touchscreen use?
•

    Does haptic feedback remove the requirement for visual feedback?
•

    Does the presence of haptic feedback affect objective touchscreen task performance?
•

    Do users express a subjective preference for touchscreen haptic feedback?

Given the visual nature of touchscreen interaction, the hypothesis is formed that degradation of visual feedback will increase the visual workload, as measured by glance time and count. This increase in visual workload is expected to have a negative effect on driving performance. With the performance benefits seen for multimodal feedback described in Section 1.1, the introduction of haptic feedback is expected to reduce visual workload by moving a proportion of this workload to the haptic channel; however as feedback is produced at the point of haptic interaction this change in workload is expected to occur in secondary visual behaviour, as defined in Section 1.2. Finally, improvements in subjective workload, confidence and liking are expected with the introduction of haptic feedback.
3. Methodology
3.1. Participants

A total of 36 subjects (18 male) were recruited. Ages ranged from 20 to 65 years, (mean = 38.1 years, SD = 12.6 years) and all participants had at least 1 year experience of driving in the UK. Subjects were recruited primarily from an existing list of participants, therefore most had some experience of simulator-based evaluation. In addition, two-thirds had experience of using touchscreen devices in their own cars, the majority of these being portable navigation devices (e.g. TomTom).
3.2. Equipment

The study was conducted using the driving simulator at the Human Factors Research Group, University of Nottingham. 1 This is a fixed-base system utilising a Honda Civic cabin and a 270° curved projection screen with three projectors providing full peripheral coverage. Separate LCD monitors display feeds for the rear- and side-view mirrors. The cabin is equipped with four miniature video cameras which capture driver behaviour. The simulation environment was generated using STISIM Drive software 2 which also recorded driver performance data.

The scenario employed for this study consisted of a simple motorway driving task. Users were required to follow a lead vehicle travelling at a constant 68 mph at what they perceived to be a ‘safe’ distance. The lead and driven vehicles were situated in the inside lane of motorway with three lanes in each direction separated by a central reservation. Traffic was included in both directions, with traffic vehicles travelling more quickly than the lead vehicle in lanes 2 and 3 while maintaining lateral lane position. Subjects were therefore not required to negotiate other vehicles and no safety-critical events such as sudden braking from the lead vehicle were included. This scenario replicates the conditions under which a driver is most likely to divert their attention to in-car tasks [26] .

The touchscreen interface consisted of a Touchsense 8.4″ demonstrator unit from Immersion Corporation, 3 a resistive touchscreen with integrated haptic feedback actuators and controller. This was securely mounted into the centre console of the vehicle, providing a close approximation of the ergonomic conditions of a factory-installed system and serving as both the input and display devices for haptic and visual stimuli generated during the touchscreen tasks. Subjects were required to perform a series of touchscreen use tasks while engaged in the driving task, described below (see Fig. 1 ).

