3.3. Evaluation interface

Previous work indicates that realistic use case tasks impose significant workload for non-expert users and that considerable training is required prior to evaluation [21] . The touchscreen task used for this study was an abstracted ‘search and select’ task whereby the participant is required to find and press a target button from a cluster displayed on the touchscreen. The interface was based on the design used by Irune and Burnett [27] to evaluate the effect of button size and cluster size on total glance time. The interface was programmed in Adobe Flash CS3 and Actionscript 3.0.

For each trial in the study, subjects were required to locate and press the button labelled “AC” from an array of buttons displayed on the touchscreen, placing the task in the context of operating the climate control system in a real vehicle. The other buttons in the array featured randomly-assigned labels which took the form “A_” or “_C”, to create a degree of visual similarity to the target; no labels were duplicated within each task ( Fig. 2 a). A successful press of the target reset the screen ready for the next task. A 3 × 3 button array was chosen, corresponding to an expected total glance time of 2–3.5 s per task [27] .

