Feedback was produced when a button was pressed. Visual feedback was presented as a change of button colour from black to white which lasted for a pre-determined period of 150 ms ( Fig. 2 b). In the delayed visual feedback condition, this occurred 1000 ms after the button press; responses within 250 ms may be considered ‘immediate’ [28] , hence a 1000 ms delay should be clearly perceptible. In order to minimise variability, one haptic feedback effect was used throughout the trial, selected on the basis of preliminary user trials. This was a simple ‘click’ effect, analogous to the feel of a mechanical pushbutton, produced on press and release of the button. Users’ affective response to touchscreen haptic feedback and task completion performance has been shown to be negatively affected by latency in haptic feedback [29] , [30] , hence the haptic stimulus was always presented without induced delay.
3.4. Experiment design

The experiment utilised a 3 × 2 repeated measures (within-subjects) design, with three levels of visual feedback: ‘immediate’, ‘delayed’, ‘none’; and two levels of haptic feedback: ‘visual only’, ‘visual + haptic’. The experiment design was counterbalanced for gender and haptic feedback order. Participants completed two drives, with and without haptic feedback, each featuring 27 touchscreen tasks encompassing all combinations of target position and visual feedback condition. Each visual feedback level was therefore experienced nine times and each target position three times. Order of presentation was randomised for each drive.

The points at which the individual tasks occurred along the roadway were programmed into the simulator scenario and signalled by an audible tone. The task was then manually initiated by the experimenter, activating the touchscreen interface. Tasks were nominally spaced at 22.5 s intervals, plus or minus a randomly-assigned value of up to 5 s, giving a minimum spacing of 17.5 s and a maximum of 27.5 s. Two versions of the scenario were used to avoid direct replication of task spacing. Additional task events were included in the scenario to allow for subjects failing to complete a task within the prescribed period, with each experimental drive lasting approximately 11 min.

In order to maintain the focus of the experiment on the feedback stimulus itself, the behaviour of the button array displayed on the screen was standardised across all visual feedback conditions. A successful press of the target button initiated a 2000 ms period within which the visual feedback stimulus would be displayed according to the experimental condition. At the end of the 2000 ms period the screen reset in preparation for the next task.

The touchscreen haptic actuators produce sound when operated that is clearly audible in bench-top operation. If uncontrolled, an auditory–haptic cross-modal effect may occur that would serve to increase the perceived amplitude of the haptic stimulus. For this study, it was not appropriate to use headphones to provide isolation due to the immersive nature of the simulator; the level of the simulated road and engine noise within the cabin was therefore adjusted such as to subjectively mask the sound emitted by the haptic actuators and negate any cross-modal influence.
3.5. Training procedure

A three-part training procedure was employed. Firstly, participants completed a consent form and check-in questionnaire to provide details about their age, driving experience and touchscreen device usage. A pre-trial simulator sickness checklist was also completed, both to provide a baseline for the subject’s condition and to make the subject aware of the possible symptoms which may be induced during interaction with the simulated environment. A second copy of this checklist was completed at the end of the study.

The second part of the training procedure was conducted inside the simulator. The participant was introduced to the touchscreen setup and was provided with a screen featuring two buttons, one with and one without haptic feedback enabled. All participants confirmed that they could perceive the haptic feedback at this stage. As the majority of participants were not from technical backgrounds, the term ‘touch feedback’ was used to refer to haptic feedback in the training and subsequent questionnaires.

The participant was then introduced to the evaluation interface and instructed on the touchscreen task as described in Section 3.3. This was followed by the completion of a series of practice tasks by the participant, featuring the different visual feedback conditions. While no strict performance criteria were set for the practice tasks, the experimenter ensured that participants were confident with the task prior to commencing the experimental drives. The programmed variations in visual feedback behaviour were not explicitly defined by the experimenter; rather participants were told that they ‘might notice some differences’ in the response of the buttons.

The final part of the training procedure involved a practice drive of approximately 6 min duration. This was conducted with the experimenter in the car to allow questions to be answered and no touchscreen tasks were conducted during this drive. Given the basic nature of the driving task, confirmation of satisfactory performance of the touchscreen task in a static condition and in order to minimise participants’ exposure to the virtual environment, a dual-task practice drive was not conducted.
3.6. Data sources

Both objective and subjective data were collected during the study. Video recording of driver behaviour allowed observation of glance count and duration, as defined by ISO 15007-1:2002 [31] . Driving performance was measured through standard deviation of lane position (SDLP), mean speed, standard deviation of speed and time headway, while touchscreen task performance was measured though task completion time.

Subjective data were collected via a questionnaire administered after each drive, with an additional follow-up questionnaire at the end of the study. Participants were asked to rate their perception of hedonic rating (how much they liked using the touchscreen), confidence in button press, touchscreen task difficulty, interference with the driving task and, for the haptic-enabled case, the strength of the haptic feedback stimulus and the realism of the feedback (how much the feedback felt like a real switch). Hedonic rating used a 9-point hedonic rating scale with semantic anchors at each point [32] , while remaining measures used 9-point scales with end and mid-point anchors. The follow-up questionnaire included a forced-choice most/least preferred selection, along with 5-point Likert scale ratings of the statements “Touch feedback makes the touchscreen easier to use” and “Touch feedback makes the screen more pleasurable to use”.
3.7. Analysis

Objective data from 35 of the 36 subjects were analysed – one data set was lost due to technical issues. Visual events were identified using video editing software to manually code actions of interest, producing an event timeline which was exported for calculation of visual behaviour metrics. This was then synchronised with driving performance and task completion data to generate the metrics defined above.

Means values for each dependent variable were calculated, taking the average across the nine repeat measurements for each visual feedback state. Differences between group means were analysed using two-way within-subjects ANOVA, with visual and haptic feedback states as factors. Significance is determined at α  = 0.05 (two-tailed) for all tests.
4. Results
4.1. Effect of haptic feedback on glance duration

As defined in Section 1.2, visual interaction with a touchscreen interface consists of primary and secondary glances. The majority of the visual workload occurs in the primary phase and one would expect to see longer glance times compared to secondary glances.

A comparison of mean primary and secondary mean glance duration is show in Fig. 3 . Note that confidence limits for glance time are too small to illustrate on the figure and error bars are therefore omitted. Student’s t -test shows no significant difference in mean primary glance time between the ‘visual only’ and the ‘visual + haptic’ states ( t (3865) = 0.50; NS). Similarly, there was no significant difference found for secondary glance time ( t (784) = 0.57; NS). Primary glances have approximately twice the duration of secondary glances on average.

