4.6. Task completion time

Task completion time is defined as the time taken from the initiation of the touchscreen task to the successful location and press of the target button. Mean task completion time for each condition was calculated and subjected to a two-way ANOVA. Only the main effect of haptic feedback was significant, showing a change in overall mean task completion time from 3.43 s to 3.09 s ( F (1, 34) = 6.938, p  < 0.05), a reduction of approximately 10%.
4.7. Driving performance

For the analysis of driving performance data were taken from the start of the task to 5 s after successful completion, thus allowing the driver time to regain their normal lane position and speed. The metrics derived were then pooled for each test condition and compared to a baseline for driving performance taken from a 3-min segment of the practice drive, where no touchscreen tasks were performed. The baseline condition was included as a fourth level in the visual feedback factor and the same values used for both haptic feedback states. A two-way ANOVA was subsequently performed to assess differences across conditions.
4.7.1. Standard deviation of lane position (SDLP)

Fig. 11 shows the results for standard deviation of lane position. Only the main effect for visual feedback was significant ( F (3, 102) = 13.901, p  < 0.001); post-hoc Tukey’s HSD confirms that differences exist only between the baseline condition and other means ( q (6, 102) = 4.10, W  = 0.140). Therefore no effect on driving performance was attributable to changes in either haptic or visual feedback, but the introduction of the touchscreen tasks caused an increase in overall mean SDLP from 18.9 cm to 24.3 cm, an increase of 28%.

