4.7.2. Speed variation

Analysis indicated that participants did not vary their speed relative to a baseline measure as a coping strategy while completing touchscreen tasks ( F (3, 102) = 0.944, NS) and that no variation was observed due haptic feedback ( F (1, 34) = 0.001, NS). Similarly, standard deviation of speed did not vary from the baseline measure ( F (3, 102) = 1.087, NS) and was not affected by haptic feedback ( F (1, 34) = 0.154, NS).
4.7.3. Headway Variation

Mean time headway during completion of the touchscreen tasks was also shown not to vary significantly from the baseline measure ( F (3, 102) = 1.388, NS); furthermore variation due to haptic feedback was not significant ( F (1, 34) = 1.424, NS). Again, this indicates that participants did not increase their distance to the lead vehicle to cope with the dual-task demands.
4.8. Order analysis

To minimise bias, the presentation order of haptic feedback state was counterbalanced with equal numbers of participants experiencing each haptic feedback condition first. By repeating the two-way ANOVA analyses with order as a factor, it was possible to confirm that there were no significant effects due to haptic feedback presentation order for glance time (primary: F (1, 34) = 0.130, NS; secondary: F (1, 34) = 0.041, NS), glance count (primary: F (1, 34) = 1.998, NS; secondary: F (1, 34) = 0.002, NS) or task completion time ( F (1, 34) = 0.014, NS).
4.9. Subjective ratings

Rating scale data from the post-trial questionnaires were analysed for significance using the non-parametric paired Wilcoxon signed-ranks test. Note that data for all 36 subjects were available for these measures. A summary of the subjective rating scale anchors used and mean scores is given in Table 2 .

