Finally, comparison between the pre- and post-study simulator sickness questionnaire responses indicated that no participants suffered detrimental effects from exposure to the simulated driving environment. Efforts made to screen participants and minimise exposure time are likely to have been beneficial in this regard.
5. Discussion

Results clearly showed that driver behaviour is affected both by the presence of a secondary task and by the types of feedback presented to them. The touchscreen interaction task used in this study was highly visual in nature, as indicated by the driver directing their gaze at the screen for over 70% of task completion time. When haptic feedback was not activated, total glance time per task increased when visual feedback was delayed or absent; degradation in visual feedback lead to an increase in visual workload. However, introducing haptic feedback counteracted this effect, with performance showing no significant difference from the optimal visual feedback condition. Therefore, while haptic feedback did not have a significant benefit when visual feedback was immediate, the total glance time per task was reduced in cases where visual feedback was delayed or absent, by 19% in the latter case.

Hypotheses formed prior to the study predicted that the effect of haptic feedback would be observed in secondary visual behaviour, i.e. after successful location and selection of the target. In fact, reductions in glance time with haptic feedback were found for both primary and secondary behaviour. Users were also able to complete more tasks with a single primary glance when haptic feedback was enabled, 46% compared to 36%. A corresponding reduction in task completion time was observed; this effect was equal for all visual feedback conditions.

The effect of delaying or removing visual feedback was to increase visual workload; this was observed as an increase in secondary glance time, as expected. Again, haptic feedback was found to counteract these effects, with no significant difference seen across visual feedback levels when haptic feedback was enabled. While the majority of tasks required no secondary glances, the probability of making a secondary glance was reduced when haptic feedback was enabled; this effect was statistically significant when visual feedback was absent, with secondary glance count reduced by 58%.

Visual workload is an important factor in the safety of an in-vehicle system. The model proposed by Wierwille [35] states that number of crashes due to visual distraction is directly proportional to glance count, and to mean glance duration to the power of 1.5. The reductions in glance time and count observed when haptic feedback was enabled will therefore have benefits in terms of safety. Perceived task difficulty and driving interference were rated ‘moderate’ or less throughout the study, indicating that the overall cognitive workload imposed by the abstracted touchscreen task and simple driving scenario were relatively low. Workload requirements, both cognitive and visual, might be expected to be significantly higher in a real-world use case, where significantly more glances and button presses may be required to complete a particular task. In such situations the premium placed upon visual attention may increase the benefits of supplying information via the haptic channel.

The expected effects of increased visual workload from a secondary task include degradation of lateral position control (i.e. increased deviation) and reduced speed [36] . Results from the study showed that standard deviation of lane position (SDLP) did indeed increase with the introduction of the secondary task, by approximately 28% relative to a baseline measure; this concurs with the findings of Rydstrom et al. [37] who observed similar results from a study involving haptically-enhanced in-car rotary controls. No changes were observed however in either speed or headway control.

Subjective responses indicate that participants expressed greater liking and were more confident in using the touchscreen with haptic feedback enabled (the latter being the stronger effect); a small but significant decrease in the perceived difficulty of the touchscreen task was also observed. As participants also ‘agree’ on average that haptic feedback makes the touchscreen both easier and more pleasurable to use, it is implied that haptic feedback improved user experience and increased driver ‘comfort’ in terms of the combined performance of driving and touchscreen interaction tasks. This is important in the wider context of the automotive market, as user controls which are perceived as ‘difficult to use’ can have a negative effect on the perception of quality of the vehicle as a whole, with a subsequent negative effect on sales [38] .

Participants’ subjective assessment of the degree of interference with the driving task showed a small but significant improvement with haptic feedback; participants felt that they their driving was less impaired when, objectively, there was no difference. Again, this adds further evidence for an overall improvement in the user experience when haptic feedback is enabled. The exclusion of auditory stimuli from the study means that relative effects of bimodal and trimodal feedback cannot be determined, but allowed a focused experiment design which generated reliable data.

The use of a simulated driving environment and abstracted secondary task impose some limitations on this study. Users may moderate their visual workload and driving behaviour differently while driving a real vehicle and more involved in-vehicle tasks may have a disproportionally greater effect on driving performance. For example, using a navigation system can produce an increase of 80% in lateral deviation [39] , compared to the 28% observed here. However, a recent study by Wang et al. [40] found that visual attention measures and task response times for technology interaction tasks conducted in a fixed-based, medium-fidelity driving simulator (similar to that used in this study) mapped closely to those from an instrumented vehicle on real roads, providing support for the validity of a simulator-based HMI evaluation approach.
6. Conclusions

This study was designed to investigate the interaction between visual and haptic feedback in a touchscreen interface. Changes observed in glance behaviour, task completion and driving performance illustrate the requirement for confirmatory feedback in this type of interaction. Delaying or removing visual feedback increased total glance time as expected; however, the introduction of haptic feedback counteracted this change with no significant difference observed from the optimal visual feedback condition. As such, haptic feedback was able to compensate for the information loss which occurred when visual feedback was degraded. The effects observed here may be more prominent in real-world use cases, where workload requirements from both driving and technology interaction are likely to be significantly higher.

Reductions in glance time and count with haptic feedback were observed in both primary and secondary measures, which will have a corresponding benefit in terms of safety as in-vehicle visual workload is correlated to accident risk. However it is important to note that the effects of secondary tasks on objective driver performance may not be mitigated by this type of haptic feedback. Touchscreen interaction still requires drivers to take their eyes off the road and their hand off the steering wheel in order to locate and perform their required function; again, the difference between baseline and in-task measures of driving performance seen in this study may be greater in a real-world scenario.

There were also additional effects attributable to haptic feedback, characterised by improved user experience and confidence in the touchscreen interface. While subjective measures of user experience may be susceptible to some degree of ‘halo’ effect from the experience of a novel technology, when viewed in conjunction with corresponding objective benefits there is compelling evidence for a tangible effect on driver comfort. Vehicle manufacturers should be aware of this when considering future HMI applications, as usability and affective response to in-vehicle technology play a major part in the perception of quality of the vehicle as a whole.

This work could be extended by the investigation of visual–haptic feedback touchscreen interaction in scenarios with varying primary and secondary task workloads. Performing further evaluations in an on-road study would provide validation of the simulator-derived results. Further analysis may be applied to existing data to examine for trends due to age, gender or technology experience.
